Assume $\epsilon_j=0$ for some $1 < j < N$. Then: $$ F_N([\epsilon]_1^N) = F_{N-j+1}([\epsilon]_{j+1}^N) $$  egin{proof} 
oindent $F$ can be written as the sum:  egin{equation} F_N = -\sum_{[Z]_1^N} P([Z]_1^{N-1}) P(Z_N | [Z]_1^{N-1}) \log P(Z_N | [Z]_1^{N-1}) \end{equation} 
oindent Here the dependence on $[\epsilon]_1^N$ and $M,T$ is hidden in the probabilities $P(..)$. Since $\epsilon_j=0$, we must have $X_j = Z_j$, and therefore (since $X$ is a Markov chain), conditioning further to the past is 'blocked', that is:  egin{equation} \epsilon_j = 0 \Rightarrow P(Z_N | [Z]_1^{N-1}) = P(Z_N | [Z]_j^{N-1}) \end{equation} 
oindent Substituting in eq. ef{F_cond_eq} gives: $$ F_N = -\sum_{[Z]_1^N} P([Z]_1^{N-1}) P(Z_N | [Z]_j^{N-1}) \log P(Z_N | [Z]_j^{N-1}) = $$  egin{equation} -\sum_{[Z]_{j}^N} P([Z]_j^{N}) \log P(Z_N | [Z]_j^{N-1}) = F_{N-j+1} \end{equation} \end{proof}

